class_name GodLore
extends RefCounted
## The stories of the gods and their realms, for the Book of the Gods. Written by tools/import_lore.py
## from Tile Shifter's legends (do not edit here: edit them there and run the tool again).

## pantheon id -> {name, subtitle, about, living (a living tradition: still practised today)}
const REALMS := {
	"greek": {"name": "Greece", "subtitle": "Realm of Olympus", "about": "The gods of ancient Greece lived on Mount Olympus, where they quarrelled, loved and meddled in human lives. Their myths, told by Homer and Hesiod, still shape art and language today.", "living": false},
	"norse": {"name": "The North", "subtitle": "Realm of Yggdrasil", "about": "The Norse gods of Asgard, known from the Eddas of medieval Iceland, live under the shadow of their fated end at Ragnarök.", "living": false},
	"egyptian": {"name": "Egypt", "subtitle": "Realm of the Duat", "about": "Ancient Egyptian religion spanned three thousand years along the Nile, centred on ma'at: truth, balance and cosmic order.", "living": false},
	"chinese": {"name": "China", "subtitle": "Realm of the Jade Emperor", "about": "Chinese myth tells of the Jade Emperor's heavenly court, the immortals, the Monkey King and the dragons of the four seas.", "living": false},
	"mayan": {"name": "The Maya", "subtitle": "Realm of the Popol Vuh", "about": "The Maya peoples of Mexico and Central America built great cities in the rain forest; their creation story, the Popol Vuh of the K'iche', tells of the gods and the Hero Twins.", "living": false},
	"mesopotamian": {"name": "Mesopotamia", "subtitle": "Realm of the Two Rivers", "about": "Between the Tigris and the Euphrates, the Sumerians, Akkadians and Babylonians built the first cities and wrote down the oldest myths in the world.", "living": false},
	"yoruba": {"name": "Yorubaland", "subtitle": "Realm of the Orisha", "about": "The Orisha of the Yoruba are honoured today by millions in Nigeria, Benin and across the Americas, from Cuba to Brazil.", "living": false},
}

## god id -> {featured (a few words), lore (the story), did_you_know}
const GODS := {
	"zeus": {"featured": "King of the gods", "lore": "Cronus swallowed each of his children at birth, for a prophecy said one of them would overthrow him. But Rhea hid her youngest, Zeus, in a cave on Crete and gave Cronus a stone wrapped in swaddling clothes instead. Grown, Zeus forced his father to bring up his brothers and sisters, and with them, and the thunderbolts forged by the Cyclopes, he overthrew the Titans in a ten-year war. From Olympus he ruled gods and mortals, the keeper of oaths, hospitality and justice.", "did_you_know": "The Olympic Games were held in his honour at Olympia, where his gold-and-ivory statue by Phidias was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."},
	"athena": {"featured": "Goddess of wisdom and war", "lore": "Athena sprang fully armed from the head of Zeus, after Hephaestus split it open with an axe to ease his terrible headache. Goddess of wisdom, crafts and the strategy of war, she preferred cunning to brute force and stood by heroes such as Odysseus, Perseus and Heracles. She won the city of Athens in a contest with Poseidon: he struck the rock and gave a salt spring, she gave the first olive tree, and the citizens chose the olive.", "did_you_know": "The Parthenon on the Acropolis was her temple. Its name comes from Athena Parthenos, \"Athena the Maiden\"."},
	"demeter": {"featured": "Goddess of the harvest", "lore": "Demeter makes the grain ripen and the earth bear fruit. When Hades carried her daughter Persephone down to the underworld, Demeter searched the whole world with burning torches, and in her grief nothing grew: the fields lay bare and people starved. Zeus ordered Hades to give Persephone back, but she had eaten a few pomegranate seeds in the underworld, and so each year she must return there for a time. While she is gone, Demeter mourns, and it is winter.", "did_you_know": "Her Mysteries at Eleusis were celebrated for nearly two thousand years. The initiates swore to keep them secret, and to this day no one knows exactly what they were shown."},
	"ares": {"featured": "God of war", "lore": "Ares, son of Zeus and Hera, is war in all its fury: the rage of battle and the thirst for blood. His sons Phobos and Deimos, Fear and Terror, ride beside him, and even the other gods did not love him. Hephaestus once caught him with Aphrodite in a net of fine chains and showed them to all Olympus; and at Troy, wounded by the hero Diomedes, he roared as loud as ten thousand men.", "did_you_know": "The two moons of Mars, named after his Roman counterpart, are called Phobos and Deimos after his sons."},
	"hermes": {"featured": "Messenger of the gods", "lore": "Born at dawn in a cave on Mount Cyllene, Hermes was up to mischief by noon. He slipped from his cradle, made the first lyre from a tortoise shell, and by evening had stolen fifty of Apollo's cattle, driving them backwards so their tracks led the wrong way. Zeus laughed, and made the clever child his herald. With winged sandals and the caduceus, Hermes carries the messages of the gods, watches over travellers, merchants and thieves, and guides the souls of the dead down to Hades.", "did_you_know": "Stone pillars topped with the head of Hermes, called herms, stood at crossroads and doorways all over Greece. When dozens were smashed in a single night in 415 BC, Athens was gripped by panic."},
	"hades": {"featured": "King of the underworld", "lore": "Hades ruled the realm of the dead with his queen Persephone, whom he carried off from a flowery meadow. Because she ate pomegranate seeds in the underworld, she must return to him for part of every year, and while she is gone the earth mourns in winter. Stern but not cruel, Hades let no soul escape, and the three-headed hound Cerberus guarded his gate. Few of the living ever entered his kingdom and came back: Orpheus, Heracles and Odysseus among them.", "did_you_know": "The Greeks often avoided saying his name and called him Plouton, \"the wealthy one\", for the riches under the earth. From it comes the Roman name Pluto."},
	"odin": {"featured": "The All-Father", "lore": "Odin, chief of the gods, gave an eye at Mímir's well for a drink of wisdom and hung nine nights on the world tree Yggdrasil to win the secret of the runes. His ravens Huginn and Muninn, Thought and Memory, fly over the world each day and tell him all they see. In Valhalla he gathers the bravest of the fallen for the last battle, Ragnarök, where he will face the wolf Fenrir.", "did_you_know": "Wednesday comes from Wōdnesdæg, \"Woden's day\": Woden is the Old English name of Odin."},
	"thor": {"featured": "God of thunder", "lore": "Thor, son of Odin, is the strongest of the gods and the defender of Asgard and Midgard against the giants. His hammer Mjölnir never misses and always returns to his hand, and thunder rolls when his chariot, drawn by the goats Tanngrisnir and Tanngnjóstr, crosses the sky. He can eat his goats at night and raise them alive in the morning by blessing their bones with Mjölnir. At Ragnarök he will slay the World Serpent, and die of its venom.", "did_you_know": "Thursday is Thor's day, and small Mjölnir pendants were worn as amulets across the Viking world."},
	"frigg": {"featured": "Queen of Asgard", "lore": "Wife of Odin and mother of Baldr, Frigg sits in her hall Fensalir and spins the clouds. She knows the fate of every being, though she never speaks of it. To protect Baldr she made everything in the world swear not to harm him, all but the little mistletoe, which seemed too young to ask. Loki made a dart of it, and Baldr fell.", "did_you_know": "Friday is named after her: in Old English it was Frīgedæg, \"Frigg's day\"."},
	"tyr": {"featured": "God of war and justice", "lore": "Týr is the bravest of the gods. When the gods bound the wolf Fenrir with the fetter Gleipnir, made of the sound of a cat's footsteps and the roots of a mountain, the wolf would only let it be put on him if one of them laid a hand in his mouth as a pledge. Only Týr dared. When Fenrir found he could not break free, he bit the hand off, and Týr has been one-handed ever since.", "did_you_know": "Tuesday is Týr's day: in Old English it was Tīwesdæg."},
	"loki": {"featured": "The trickster", "lore": "Son of a giant yet counted among the gods, Loki is clever, charming and never to be trusted. His tricks bring the gods endless trouble, and his wits save them as often: he gave birth to Odin's eight-legged horse Sleipnir and won the gods their greatest treasures, Thor's hammer among them. But after he caused the death of Baldr, the gods bound him beneath a serpent dripping venom, where he will lie until Ragnarök.", "did_you_know": "Loki's children are the wolf Fenrir, the World Serpent Jörmungandr and Hel, who rules the dead."},
	"hel": {"featured": "Queen of the dead", "lore": "Hel, daughter of Loki and the giantess Angrboða, is half pale as the living and half dark as the dead. Odin cast her into Niflheim and gave her rule over all who die of sickness and old age. Her hall is Éljúðnir, beyond the river Gjöll and its golden bridge, guarded by the maiden Móðguðr. At Ragnarök, the dead of her realm will sail with Loki against the gods.", "did_you_know": "The English word \"hell\" comes from the same old Germanic word as her name, meaning a hidden place."},
	"ra": {"featured": "The sun god", "lore": "Ra, the falcon-headed sun god, crosses the sky each day in his solar barque, bringing light to the world. Each night he sails through the underworld, where the great serpent Apep tries to swallow the sun, and each dawn he is born again victorious in the east. Creator and king of the gods, he was so honoured that every pharaoh called himself \"Son of Ra\".", "did_you_know": "Heliopolis, the \"city of the sun\", was his great temple city; obelisks from it now stand in Rome, London and New York."},
	"isis": {"featured": "Great of magic", "lore": "Isis, the greatest of magicians, was the devoted wife of Osiris. When his jealous brother Set murdered him and scattered his body across Egypt, she searched the whole land, gathered the pieces and with her magic brought him back to life long enough to conceive their son Horus. She hid the child in the papyrus marshes of the Delta until he was old enough to claim his father's throne.", "did_you_know": "Her worship spread across the Roman Empire: there were temples of Isis as far away as Pompeii and Roman London."},
	"ptah": {"featured": "Creator and craftsman", "lore": "Ptah, the great god of Memphis, created the world by thought and word: he conceived all things in his heart and brought them into being by speaking their names. Patron of craftsmen, builders and sculptors, he was honoured by every artisan who carved a statue or raised a temple. The sacred Apis bull was his living image on earth.", "did_you_know": "The Greek name for Egypt, Aigyptos, may come from Hikuptah, \"house of the spirit of Ptah\", the name of his temple at Memphis."},
	"sekhmet": {"featured": "The lioness of the sun", "lore": "Sekhmet, the lioness, is the burning Eye of Ra. When mankind plotted against the ageing sun god, he sent her to punish them, and she rampaged until the land ran with blood. To save humanity, Ra had seven thousand jars of beer dyed red and poured over the fields. Sekhmet drank it, thinking it was blood, fell asleep, and woke calm. She could send plagues, and so her priests were also Egypt's doctors.", "did_you_know": "Pharaoh Amenhotep III had hundreds of statues of Sekhmet carved for his temples at Thebes; many now stand in museums around the world."},
	"set": {"featured": "Lord of the desert and storm", "lore": "Set, god of the desert, storms and foreign lands, murdered his brother Osiris and fought Horus for eighty years for the throne of Egypt. Yet he was not only an enemy: each night he stood at the prow of the sun boat and speared the serpent Apep, who tried to swallow the sun, so that Ra could rise again. He was the god of strength, and of the red desert beyond the black land of the Nile.", "did_you_know": "Great pharaohs were named after him: Seti I, father of Ramesses the Great, means \"man of Set\"."},
	"anubis": {"featured": "Guardian of the dead", "lore": "Long before the pyramids, jackals prowled the desert cemeteries of Egypt. The Egyptians turned that fear into a protector: Anubis, the jackal-headed god of embalming and guardian of the necropolis. It was Anubis who prepared the dead for eternity and led them through the Duat, the perilous underworld, to the Hall of Two Truths, where he watched over the scales as the heart was weighed against the feather of Ma'at.", "did_you_know": "Anubis is painted black not for death but for the fertile black silt of the Nile flood: the colour of rebirth."},
	"jade_emperor": {"featured": "Ruler of heaven", "lore": "The Jade Emperor rules heaven from his celestial palace like an emperor on earth, with a court of gods, officials and generals. Every god and spirit answers to him, and each New Year the Kitchen God rises to heaven to report to him on every household. In legend he chose the animals of the zodiac by holding a great race.", "did_you_know": "His birthday is celebrated on the ninth day of the first lunar month, especially in Fujian and Taiwan."},
	"guanyin": {"featured": "Goddess of mercy", "lore": "Guanyin, the bodhisattva of compassion, hears the cries of all who suffer and comes to their aid. Her sanctuary is the island of Mount Putuo in the eastern sea, and she carries a vase of pure water and a willow branch to pour blessings over the world. In Journey to the West, she chooses the disciples who will protect the monk Tang Sanzang, and gives him the golden headband that tames the Monkey King.", "did_you_know": "Her name means \"the one who perceives the cries of the world\"."},
	"guan_yu": {"featured": "God of loyalty", "lore": "Guan Yu was a general of the Three Kingdoms period, who died in 220 AD. In the Peach Garden he swore brotherhood with Liu Bei and Zhang Fei, and he kept that oath faithfully through every battle and temptation. With his red face, long beard and Green Dragon Crescent Blade, he became the model of loyalty and honour, and was worshipped after his death as Guan Di.", "did_you_know": "Shrines to Guan Yu stand in police stations, businesses and homes, where he guards loyalty and fair dealing."},
	"sun_wukong": {"featured": "The Monkey King", "lore": "Born from a stone on the Mountain of Flowers and Fruit, Sun Wukong learned seventy-two transformations and could leap a hundred thousand li in a single somersault. He called himself the Great Sage Equal to Heaven, ate the peaches of immortality and fought all the armies of heaven, until the Buddha trapped him under a mountain for five hundred years. Freed, he guarded the monk Tang Sanzang on his long journey to India for the sacred scriptures.", "did_you_know": "His story comes from the 16th-century novel Journey to the West, one of the Four Great Classical Novels of Chinese literature."},
	"nezha": {"featured": "The boy god of the lotus", "lore": "Nezha, son of the general Li Jing, was born after three and a half years as a ball of flesh, and grew into a fearless child warrior. Playing by the sea, he fought and killed the son of the Dragon King of the Eastern Sea. To spare his parents the dragons' revenge, he gave back his flesh and bones, and his master rebuilt him a body from lotus flowers. He rides on wind-fire wheels with his fire-tipped spear and his red silk sash.", "did_you_know": "The animated film Ne Zha 2 (2025) became the highest-grossing animated film of all time."},
	"hou_yi": {"featured": "The archer who shot the suns", "lore": "Long ago, ten suns, the sons of the god Di Jun, rose into the sky together and scorched the earth until the crops withered and the rivers dried up. The great archer Hou Yi drew his red bow and shot down nine of them, leaving one to light the world. He also slew the monsters that plagued the land. The Queen Mother of the West rewarded him with the elixir of immortality, which his wife Chang'e drank.", "did_you_know": "In some tellings the suns he shot fell to earth as golden three-legged crows, the birds that live in the sun."},
	"itzamna": {"featured": "Lord of the sky", "lore": "Itzamna, the old creator god of the ancient Maya, was lord of the heavens, of day and night. He was a priest and a healer, and the Maya believed he had given them writing and the books in which they recorded the movements of the sky. He is shown as a wise old man on a throne in the sky, with a great bird perched above him.", "did_you_know": "Only four Maya books survived the Spanish conquest. One of them, the Dresden Codex, holds tables that follow the planet Venus for over a century."},
	"chaak": {"featured": "God of rain", "lore": "Chaak, the rain god, strikes the clouds with his lightning axe to bring rain to the fields of maize. There are four Chaaks, one for each direction of the world, each with his own colour. In a land of cenotes and dry seasons, the farmers' lives depended on his favour, and his long-nosed face covers the walls of many temples in Yucatán.", "did_you_know": "In Yucatán, farmers still hold the Ch'a Chaak ceremony in times of drought to call the rain."},
	"ix_chel": {"featured": "Lady of the moon", "lore": "Ix Chel, the moon goddess, watched over weaving, medicine and childbirth. Women made pilgrimages by canoe to her shrines on the islands of Cozumel and Isla Mujeres to ask for her blessing. She is often shown at her backstrap loom, weaving the cloth of the world, with a rabbit, the creature the Maya saw in the moon.", "did_you_know": "Where some see a man in the moon, the Maya saw a rabbit; and Maya women still weave on backstrap looms like the one Ix Chel holds."},
	"kukulkan": {"featured": "The feathered serpent", "lore": "Kukulkan, the feathered serpent, was a god of wind, rain and kingship, worshipped across Yucatán, above all at Chichén Itzá. His great pyramid there, El Castillo, has a stairway on each side, and stone serpent heads lie at the foot of the northern one.", "did_you_know": "At the spring and autumn equinoxes, the setting sun makes a pattern of triangles of light on the steps of El Castillo, like a serpent slithering down to its stone head."},
	"huracan": {"featured": "Heart of Sky", "lore": "Huracan, the one-legged god of wind, storm and fire, is the Heart of Sky of the Popol Vuh. With the other creators he raised the earth out of the sea; when the first people, carved from wood, forgot their makers, he sent a great flood and a black rain to wipe them away.", "did_you_know": "The word \"hurricane\" came into English through Spanish from the Taíno huracán of the Caribbean, a name very like his."},
	"ah_puch": {"featured": "Lord of death", "lore": "Ah Puch, also called Kimi, is the god of death, a skeleton decked with bells, lord of Xibalba, the place of fear. Owls were his messengers. Xibalba lay deep beneath the earth, and the Maya thought it could be reached through caves and the waters of the cenotes.", "did_you_know": "The great cave of Actun Tunichil Muknal in Belize, with offerings left by the ancient Maya, is thought to have been one of the entrances to Xibalba."},
	"marduk": {"featured": "Champion of the gods", "lore": "Marduk, the god of Babylon, agreed to fight Tiamat if the gods made him their king. He rode out in his storm chariot, caught her in his net, sent the winds into her and split her in two: from one half he made the sky and from the other the earth.", "did_you_know": "His great ziggurat in Babylon, Etemenanki, may have inspired the story of the Tower of Babel."},
	"enki": {"featured": "God of wisdom and fresh water", "lore": "Enki, called Ea in Akkadian, lived in the Abzu, the sweet waters under the earth. He was the god of wisdom, magic and craft, and the friend of humankind: when the gods sent the great flood, he warned a wise man to build a boat and save every kind of living thing.", "did_you_know": "Eridu, his city, was thought by the Sumerians to be the first city on earth; its temple was rebuilt on the same spot for thousands of years."},
	"utu": {"featured": "The sun and the judge", "lore": "Utu, called Shamash in Akkadian, rises each morning from between the eastern mountains, his rays streaming from his shoulders, and crosses the sky. Seeing all that happens on earth, he is the god of justice, who protects the wronged and judges the wicked.", "did_you_know": "The law code of Hammurabi, carved on a black stone pillar now in the Louvre, shows the king standing before Shamash, the god of justice."},
	"inanna": {"featured": "Queen of Heaven", "lore": "Inanna, called Ishtar in Akkadian, the Queen of Heaven, goddess of love and war, is the morning and evening star. She went down to the underworld of her sister Ereshkigal, giving up a crown, a jewel or a robe at each of its seven gates, and came back only with the help of the god Enki.", "did_you_know": "The Ishtar Gate of Babylon, faced with blue glazed bricks, golden lions, bulls and dragons, has been rebuilt in the Pergamon Museum in Berlin."},
	"ereshkigal": {"featured": "Queen of the Great Below", "lore": "Ereshkigal rules the land of no return, the underworld where the dead dwell in the dusk. When her sister Inanna came down to her kingdom, she judged her and held her there, for no one, not even a goddess, may leave the underworld without leaving another in her place.", "did_you_know": "In another myth she marries Nergal, the god of plague, who comes down to her kingdom and stays."},
	"tiamat": {"featured": "The primeval sea", "lore": "In the beginning, says the Babylonian Enuma Elish, the salt sea Tiamat and the fresh water Apsu mingled, and from them the gods were born. When the young gods killed Apsu, Tiamat raised an army of monsters, scorpion-men, dragons and serpents, to destroy them.", "did_you_know": "The Enuma Elish was recited in Babylon each spring, during the festival of the New Year."},
	"shango": {"featured": "Lord of thunder", "lore": "Shango, the Orisha of thunder, lightning and fire, was once the fourth Alaafin, the king of Oyo. He breathes fire, hurls thunderstones from the sky and rides a white horse; he punishes liars and thieves, and his drums, the bata, sound in his honour.", "did_you_know": "His dancers carry the oshe, a staff topped with a double axe that stands for the thunderstones he hurls."},
	"yemoja": {"featured": "Mother of the waters", "lore": "Yemoja is the mother of many of the Orisha, and of all living things that come from the waters. In Yorubaland she is the Orisha of the Ogun river; across the ocean, where her children were carried, she became the mother of the sea, Yemaya or Iemanjá.", "did_you_know": "Every 2 February in Salvador, Brazil, thousands of people bring flowers and gifts to the sea for Iemanjá."},
	"obatala": {"featured": "King of the white cloth", "lore": "Obatala, the Orisha of purity, wisdom and calm, came down from the sky on a golden chain with a snail shell full of earth and a five-toed hen. The hen scattered the earth over the water and made the first dry land, at Ile-Ife; and Obatala shaped the bodies of human beings from clay.", "did_you_know": "His devotees dress in white; in one story he shaped people while drunk on palm wine, so his followers avoid it, and he protects all who are born different."},
	"ogun": {"featured": "Lord of iron", "lore": "When the Orisha came down to the world, the primeval forest blocked their way; only Ogun, with his iron machete, could clear a path for them. He is the Orisha of iron, of war and of work, patron of smiths and hunters, and of all who use tools.", "did_you_know": "Today drivers, mechanics and surgeons also call on Ogun, as all who work with metal."},
	"oya": {"featured": "Queen of the winds", "lore": "Oya, the warrior Orisha of winds, storms and change, is Shango's wife and fights beside him with lightning. She can take the form of a water buffalo, she guards the marketplace, and she stands at the gate between the living and the dead.", "did_you_know": "The Yoruba name of the River Niger is Odo Oya, the river of Oya."},
	"esu": {"featured": "Messenger of the crossroads", "lore": "Esu is the messenger of the Orisha and the keeper of the crossroads. He carries offerings and prayers between people and the Orisha, and he guards the road between choices. He is a trickster who teaches that every act has its consequence; he is not a devil, but the one who opens and closes the way.", "did_you_know": "Many Orisha ceremonies begin with an offering to Esu, so that the way may be open."},
}


static func realm(pantheon_id: String) -> Dictionary:
	return REALMS.get(pantheon_id, {})


static func god(god_id: String) -> Dictionary:
	return GODS.get(god_id, {})
